================================================================================
Third-party data: fec-csv-sources format tables
================================================================================

The files under `data/fec-csv-sources/*.csv` -- the column-position tables
that map each FEC electronic filing spec version to canonical field names --
are derived from the `fech-sources` project:

    https://github.com/dwillis/fech-sources

That repository's own README states these files "are the same files in the
sources directory in earlier version[s] of Fech itself," i.e. they originate
from the `sources/` directory of the Fech Ruby gem (the NYT's FEC filing
parser, later maintained by Derek Willis):

    https://github.com/dwillis/Fech

The `fech-sources` GitHub repository does not itself carry a LICENSE file.
However, the Fech gem it was extracted from is licensed Apache-2.0, per its
official RubyGems.org package metadata:

    https://rubygems.org/api/v1/gems/fech.json  ->  "licenses": ["Apache-2.0"]

hardmoney treats the vendored data/fec-csv-sources/ tables as Apache-2.0
licensed on that basis, and includes this NOTICE and attribution as required
by Apache License 2.0, Section 4(d).

Modifications made by hardmoney to this vendored data (Apache License 2.0,
Section 4(b) requires stating changes):

  A comprehensive audit (see `tests/format_table_integrity.rs`) found that
  six of the vendored format tables assigned the same canonical field name
  to two different column positions within a single FEC spec version
  bucket. Because hardmoney's line parser (src/parser/line.rs) builds an
  ordered map of canonical-name -> value per line, an unresolved collision
  meant the second-occurring field silently overwrote the first, discarding
  real data present in the underlying filing. This was confirmed against
  real filings (e.g. tests/fixtures/F3XN_2011834.fec), where the two
  colliding fields held different real dollar values.

  The following canonical field names were renamed to be unique and
  form-accurate, resolving the collisions with no loss of information:

  F3X.csv (FEC Form 3X, Report of Receipts and Disbursements):
    - Line 19/31/33 "detailed summary recap" fields renamed with a
      `_recap` suffix, disambiguating them from the identically-labeled
      totals used elsewhere on the same form:
        col_a_total_receipts_recap, col_a_total_disbursements_recap,
        col_a_total_contributions_recap, col_b_total_receipts_recap,
        col_b_total_disbursements_recap, col_b_total_contributions_recap
    - Line 16 "Refunds of Federal Contributions" (receipts side) and
      Line 34 "Total Contribution Refunds" (disbursement-recap side) were
      both mapped to the same canonical name in the source data; renamed
      the Line 16 occurrences to:
        col_a_refunds_of_federal_contributions,
        col_b_refunds_of_federal_contributions
      leaving col_a_total_contributions_refunds /
      col_b_total_contributions_refunds uniquely referring to Line 34.

  F3P.csv (FEC Form 3P, Presidential) and F4.csv (FEC Form 4, PAC/Party
  non-authorized):
    - Same "detailed summary recap" pattern as F3X; renamed the second
      occurrence of the receipts/disbursements totals to
      col_a_total_receipts_recap, col_a_total_disbursements_recap
      (F4 additionally has col_b_total_receipts_recap,
      col_b_total_disbursements_recap).

  F2.csv (FEC Form 2, Statement of Candidacy):
    - Two distinct fields -- the mailing-address state and the
      office-sought state -- were both mapped to canonical name
      `candidate_state`. Renamed the office-sought occurrence to
      `candidate_office_state`.

  SchC1.csv (Schedule C-1, Loan/Line of Credit Report):
    - Two distinct "description" fields (Section D.1 Collateral vs.
      Section E.2 Future Income) renamed to `collateral_description` and
      `future_income_description`.
    - Two distinct "date_signed" fields (Section G Treasurer vs.
      Section H Authorized Individual) renamed to `treasurer_date_signed`
      and `authorized_date_signed`.

  SchL.csv (Schedule L, Levin Funds):
    - Two columns were mislabeled as belonging to Column B when they
      structurally belong to Column A's own per-period recap section
      (immediately following the genuine col_a_receipts_period /
      col_a_subtotal_period fields). Corrected to
      col_a_disbursements_period and col_a_cash_on_hand_close_of_period.
      The genuine Column B fields elsewhere in the table are unaffected.

  No numeric or textual filing data was altered by any of the above --
  only the canonical field *names* used to key into each parsed line's
  field map.

================================================================================
Inspiration, not copied code
================================================================================

hardmoney's FEC-parsing logic (header/version detection, delimiter
handling, line dispatch, field typing) is an original implementation
written for this project. In designing it, ideas and general approach were
informed by reviewing publicly available open-source FEC parsers,
including:

  - nyt-pyfec (The New York Times), Apache License 2.0,
    Copyright 2015 The New York Times Company
    https://github.com/newsdev/nyt-pyfec
    License: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/newsdev/nyt-pyfec/master/LICENSE

No source code from nyt-pyfec or any other third-party FEC parser was
copied into hardmoney; only the fec-csv-sources data tables described
above were vendored, under the terms above.
